// Class 11 Physics Unit VI: Gravitation (100% Exact Official NCERT Syllabus Alignment)
// High-Level Reference Book Content & NCERT Solutions (6 Self-Contained 3D Volumetric SVG Diagrams)

export const c11Phy6HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY & BASIC DEFINITIONS -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📖 Quick Glossary & Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Terms for Unit VI: Gravitation</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>1. Gravitation:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The universal force of mutual attraction acting between any two bodies in the universe by virtue of their masses.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>2. Acceleration due to Gravity (g):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The acceleration produced in a freely falling body under the influence of Earth's gravitational pull (g ≈ 9.8 m/s² on Earth's surface).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>3. Gravitational Field Intensity (E):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The gravitational force experienced per unit mass placed at a given point in space: E = F / m = G M / r².</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>4. Gravitational Potential (V):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The amount of work done in bringing a unit mass from infinity to a point in gravitational field: V = - G M / r.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>5. Gravitational Potential Energy (U):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The work done in bringing a mass m from infinity to distance r in a gravitational field: U = - G M m / r.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>6. Escape Speed (v_e):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The minimum velocity required for a body to be projected from Earth's surface to escape Earth's gravitational field completely (v_e ≈ 11.2 km/s).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>7. Orbital Velocity (v_o):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The horizontal speed required for a satellite to revolve in a stable circular orbit around a planet (v_o = √(g R) ≈ 7.92 km/s near Earth).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>8. Geostationary Satellite:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">A satellite revolving in the equatorial plane with an orbital period equal to Earth's rotational period (24 hours) at height ~35,786 km.</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1: KEPLER'S LAWS OF PLANETARY MOTION (100% OFFICIAL SYLLABUS TITLE) -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">1. Kepler's Laws of Planetary Motion</h2>\n  \n  <p>Johannes Kepler formulated three empirical laws governing planetary motion based on Tycho Brahe's detailed astronomical observations. These laws laid the foundation for Isaac Newton to derive the Universal Law of Gravitation.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 18px;\">(i) First Law - Law of Orbits</h3>\n  <p>Every planet revolves around the Sun in an <b>elliptical orbit</b>, with the Sun situated at one of the two foci of the ellipse. The point of closest approach to the Sun is called <b>perihelion</b>, while the point of farthest distance is called <b>aphelion</b>.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 18px;\">(ii) Second Law - Law of Areas</h3>\n  <p>The radius vector drawn from the Sun to a planet sweeps out <b>equal areas in equal intervals of time</b>. This implies that the areal velocity of a planet remains constant:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dA</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 m</span>\n  </span> = Constant</b></div>\n  <p>The Law of Areas is a direct consequence of the <b>Conservation of Angular Momentum</b> because the gravitational force exerted by the Sun is a central force, exerting zero torque ($\tau = 0$) on the planet.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 18px;\">(iii) Third Law - Law of Periods</h3>\n  <p>The square of the period of revolution ($T$) of any planet around the Sun is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis ($a$) of its elliptical orbit:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>T<sup>2</sup> ∝ a<sup>3</sup> &nbsp; ⇒ &nbsp; T<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 π<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G M<sub>s</sub></span>\n  </span> a<sup>3</sup></b></div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 16px; padding: 18px; margin: 26px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 14px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 Kepler's Laws of Planetary Motion (Elliptical Orbit & Swept Areas)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 840px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 840 480\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"sunGrad1\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FFFDE7\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"30%\" stop-color=\"#FDC830\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"85%\" stop-color=\"#F39C12\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#E67E22\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <radialGradient id=\"planetGrad1\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#E0F7FA\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"40%\" stop-color=\"#00E5FF\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#0284C7\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <linearGradient id=\"areaGrad1\" x1=\"0%\" y1=\"0%\" x2=\"100%\" y2=\"100%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.4)\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.05)\"/>\n    </linearGradient>\n    <filter id=\"glowGold1\" x=\"-20%\" y=\"-20%\" width=\"140%\" height=\"140%\">\n      <feGaussianBlur stdDeviation=\"6\" result=\"blur\"/>\n      <feComposite in=\"SourceGraphic\" in2=\"blur\" operator=\"over\"/>\n    </filter>\n  </defs>\n\n  <!-- Left Side: Elliptical Orbit Diagram (Width 390, Center x = 200) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 20)\">\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Kepler's Orbits &amp; Swept Area Law</text>\n    <ellipse cx=\"195\" cy=\"220\" rx=\"145\" ry=\"90\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"125\" cy=\"220\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#sunGrad1)\" filter=\"url(#glowGold1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"125\" y=\"255\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sun (Focus F₁)</text>\n    <circle cx=\"265\" cy=\"220\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#94A3B8\"/>\n    <text x=\"265\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\">Focus F₂</text>\n\n    <!-- Swept Area -->\n    <path d=\"M 125 220 L 50 220 A 145 90 0 0 1 80 145 Z\" fill=\"url(#areaGrad1)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"75\" y=\"195\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Area A₁</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"220\" r=\"9\" fill=\"url(#planetGrad1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Perihelion (v₁ max)</text>\n    <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"220\" r=\"9\" fill=\"url(#planetGrad1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"340\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Aphelion (v₂ min)</text>\n\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Constant Areal Velocity dA/dt = L / (2 m)</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <!-- Vertical Divider -->\n  <line x1=\"415\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"415\" y2=\"450\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <!-- Right Side: Laws Summary Card (Width 390, Center x = 620) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(430, 20)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"390\" height=\"430\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.9)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"35\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Summary of Kepler's 3 Laws</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"60\" width=\"360\" height=\"100\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(253,200,48,0.08)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"85\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">First Law (Law of Orbits):</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\">Planets revolve in elliptical orbits with the Sun at one focus.</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"175\" width=\"360\" height=\"105\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.08)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Second Law (Law of Areas):</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\">A radius vector sweeps equal areas in equal intervals of time.</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"255\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"13\">dA / dt = L / (2 m) = Constant</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"295\" width=\"360\" height=\"115\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(118,255,3,0.08)\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Third Law (Law of Periods):</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\">Square of orbital period T is proportional to cube of semi-major axis a.</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">T² = (4π² / GM) a³  ⇒  T² ∝ a³</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 3D Elliptical orbit showing Sun at focus F₁, swept area A₁, perihelion, aphelion, and areal velocity conservation.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: UNIVERSAL LAW OF GRAVITATION (100% OFFICIAL SYLLABUS TITLE) -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">2. Universal Law of Gravitation</h2>\n\n  <p>Sir Isaac Newton stated that every particle of matter in the universe attracts every other particle with a force that is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>F = G <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m<sub>1</sub> m<sub>2</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; G = 6.674 × 10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup></b></div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 18px;\">(i) Vector Form & Properties of Gravitational Force</h3>\n  <p>In vector notation, the gravitational force exerted by mass $m_1$ on mass $m_2$ separated by position vector $mathbf{r}_{12}$ is given by:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>F<sub>12</sub> = - G <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m<sub>1</sub> m<sub>2</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> r̂<sub>12</sub> &nbsp; ⇒ &nbsp; F<sub>12</sub> = - F<sub>21</sub></b></div>\n  <p>Gravitational forces form an action-reaction pair conforming strictly to Newton's Third Law of Motion. It is a central, conservative force operating along the line joining the centers of mass of the interacting bodies.</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 16px; padding: 18px; margin: 26px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 14px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 Universal Law of Gravitation Vector Model\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 840px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 840 480\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"m1Grad\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FF8A65\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#DD2476\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <radialGradient id=\"m2Grad\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#8E2DE2\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#4A00E0\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <marker id=\"arr1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z\" fill=\"#FDC830\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 20)\">\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Gravitational Attraction Vector Model</text>\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"210\" r=\"38\" fill=\"url(#m1Grad)\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"216\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">m₁</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"210\" r=\"28\" fill=\"url(#m2Grad)\"/>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">m₂</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"138\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#arr1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"165\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">F₁₂ →</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"262\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#arr1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"235\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">← F₂₁</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\"/>\n    <text x=\"200\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\">Center Distance r</text>\n\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Action-Reaction Pair: F₁₂ = - F₂₁</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"415\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"415\" y2=\"450\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(430, 20)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"390\" height=\"430\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.9)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"35\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Law Formulae &amp; Universal Constant</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"60\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(253,200,48,0.08)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"85\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Gravitational Force Equation:</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\">F = G (m₁ m₂ / r²)</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\">✓ Inverse Square Law: Force drops as 1/r²</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"185\" width=\"360\" height=\"115\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(118,255,3,0.08)\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Universal Gravitational Constant G:</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">G = 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻²</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"275\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"13\">Dimensions of G: [M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²]</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"310\" width=\"360\" height=\"105\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.08)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"335\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Properties of Gravitational Force:</text>\n    <text x=\"25\" y=\"362\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">• Always attractive, independent of medium between masses</text>\n    <text x=\"25\" y=\"385\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">• Central force operating along line joining mass centers</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Mutual gravitational attraction vectors F₁₂ and F₂₁ between masses m₁ and m₂ obeying inverse square law.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: ACCELERATION DUE TO GRAVITY AND ITS VARIATION WITH ALTITUDE AND DEPTH (100% OFFICIAL SYLLABUS TITLE) -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">3. Acceleration Due to Gravity and Its Variation with Altitude and Depth</h2>\n\n  <p>The acceleration produced in a freely falling body due to Earth's gravitational pull is called acceleration due to gravity ($g$). On Earth's surface, assuming Earth to be a uniform sphere of mass $M$ and radius $R$:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>g = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">3</span>\n  </span> π G ρ R ≈ 9.8 m s<sup>-2</sup></b></div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 18px;\">(i) Variation of g with Altitude (Height h above Earth Surface)</h3>\n  <p>At height $h$ above Earth's surface, distance from Earth's center is $r = R + h$. The value of $g_h$ becomes:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>g<sub>h</sub> = g ( 1 - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 h</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span>\n  </span> ) &nbsp; (Valid for h << R)</b></div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 18px;\">(ii) Variation of g with Depth (Depth d below Earth Surface)</h3>\n  <p>At depth $d$ below Earth's surface, only the inner sphere of radius $(R - d)$ contributes to gravitational pull. Thus:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>g<sub>d</sub> = g ( 1 - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span>\n  </span> )</b></div>\n  <p>At Earth's center ($d = R$), acceleration due to gravity becomes zero ($g_{\text{center}} = 0$).</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 16px; padding: 18px; margin: 26px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 14px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 Variation of g with Altitude & Depth Geometry\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 840px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 840 480\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 20)\">\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Earth Geometry: Altitude h &amp; Depth d</text>\n    <circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"220\" r=\"110\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.08)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"220\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#FFD700\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\">Earth Center (g = 0)</text>\n\n    <!-- Altitude Line -->\n    <line x1=\"195\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"70\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n    <text x=\"205\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Point P (Height h)</text>\n    <text x=\"205\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\">Radius R</text>\n\n    <!-- Depth Line -->\n    <circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"160\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    <text x=\"205\" y=\"165\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Point Q (Depth d)</text>\n\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Surface value g = GM / R² ≈ 9.8 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"415\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"415\" y2=\"450\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(430, 20)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"390\" height=\"430\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.9)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"35\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Equations for Variation of g</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"60\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(118,255,3,0.08)\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"85\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Variation with Altitude h:</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"15.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">g_h = g (1 - 2h / R) &nbsp; (For h &lt;&lt; R)</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">✓ General formula: g_h = g [R / (R + h)]²</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"185\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.08)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Variation with Depth d:</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">g_d = g (1 - d / R)</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"275\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">✓ At Earth's center (d = R): g_center = 0</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"310\" width=\"360\" height=\"105\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(253,200,48,0.08)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"335\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Latitude &amp; Rotation Variation:</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"365\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"14\">g_λ = g - ω² R cos² λ</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"390\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\">• Max at Poles (λ = 90°), Min at Equator (λ = 0°)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Spherical Earth geometry illustrating acceleration due to gravity variations at height h, depth d, and center g=0.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL ENERGY AND GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL (100% OFFICIAL SYLLABUS TITLE) -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">4. Gravitational Potential Energy and Gravitational Potential</h2>\n\n  <p>Gravitational field is a conservative field. The gravitational potential energy of a system of masses is defined as the work done by an external force in bringing the masses from infinity to their present positions without acceleration.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>V(r) = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span>\n  </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; U(r) = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G M m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span>\n  </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; E = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dV</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dr</span>\n  </span></b></div>\n\n  <p>The negative sign indicates that the gravitational force is attractive in nature and work is done by the gravitational field when masses approach each other.</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 16px; padding: 18px; margin: 26px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 14px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 Gravitational Potential Energy U(r) Potential Well Graph\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 840px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 840 480\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 20)\">\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Potential Well Graph U(r) vs Distance r</text>\n    <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"330\" x2=\"350\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"#475569\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"350\" stroke=\"#475569\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"355\" y=\"335\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\">Distance r</text>\n\n    <!-- Hyperbolic Curve U = -G M m / r -->\n    <path d=\"M 75 320 Q 110 280 180 180 Q 260 120 340 90\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"180\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#00E5FF\"/>\n    <text x=\"190\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">U = - GMm / r</text>\n\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Zero Potential at Infinity (r = ∞)</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"415\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"415\" y2=\"450\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(430, 20)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"390\" height=\"430\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.9)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"35\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Potential &amp; Energy Equations</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"60\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(253,200,48,0.08)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"85\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Gravitational Potential V(r):</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\">V = - G M / r &nbsp; [J kg⁻¹]</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">✓ Scalar field representing work done per unit mass</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"185\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(118,255,3,0.08)\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Gravitational Potential Energy U(r):</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\">U = m V = - G M m / r</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"275\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">✓ Negative sign signifies attractive bound system</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"310\" width=\"360\" height=\"105\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.08)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"335\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Relation between Intensity E &amp; V:</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"365\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"15.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">E = - dV / dr</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Hyperbolic potential well curve U(r) = -GMm/r showing bound system energy and zero potential at infinity.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5: ESCAPE SPEED (100% OFFICIAL SYLLABUS TITLE) -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">5. Escape Speed</h2>\n\n  <p>Escape speed is the minimum initial speed with which a body must be projected from Earth's surface so that it overcomes Earth's gravitational field and never returns back on its own.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>v<sub>e</sub> = √(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 G M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span>\n  </span>) = √(2 g R) ≈ 11.2 km s<sup>-1</sup> &nbsp; (On Earth Surface)</b></div>\n\n  <p>Escape speed depends only on the mass and radius of the celestial body and is completely independent of the mass of the projected object or its angle of projection.</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 16px; padding: 18px; margin: 26px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 14px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 Escape Velocity Trajectory & Energy Conservation\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 840px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 840 480\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"earthGrad5\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#81D4FA\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"50%\" stop-color=\"#0284C7\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#0F172A\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <marker id=\"arr5\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 20)\">\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Escape Velocity Trajectory to Infinity</text>\n    <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"250\" r=\"75\" fill=\"url(#earthGrad5)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"130\" y=\"255\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Earth M</text>\n\n    <!-- Parabolic Escape Path -->\n    <path d=\"M 130 175 Q 180 120 340 70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr5)\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">v_e → ∞</text>\n\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Total Mechanical Energy E_total ≥ 0</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"415\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"415\" y2=\"450\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(430, 20)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"390\" height=\"430\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.9)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"35\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Escape Speed Formula &amp; Values</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"60\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(253,200,48,0.08)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"85\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Energy Conservation Principle:</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">½ m v_e² - (G M m / R) = 0</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">v_e = √(2 G M / R) = √(2 g R)</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"185\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.08)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Earth's Escape Speed Value:</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\">v_e ≈ 11.2 km/s</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"275\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">✓ Independent of mass and angle of projection!</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"310\" width=\"360\" height=\"105\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(118,255,3,0.08)\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"335\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Moon's Escape Speed (v_e ≈ 2.38 km/s):</text>\n    <text x=\"25\" y=\"362\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">• Moon's v_e is less than thermal speeds of air molecules</text>\n    <text x=\"25\" y=\"385\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">• Hence, the Moon has no atmosphere!</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Parabolic escape trajectory showing energy balance 1/2 m v_e² = G M m / R yielding v_e ≈ 11.2 km/s.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6: ORBITAL VELOCITY OF A SATELLITE (100% OFFICIAL SYLLABUS TITLE) -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">6. Orbital Velocity of a Satellite</h2>\n\n  <p>Orbital velocity ($v_o$) is the velocity required for a satellite to revolve in a stable circular orbit around a planet. The necessary centripetal force is provided by the gravitational attraction between the planet and satellite.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>v<sub>o</sub> = √(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R + h</span>\n  </span>) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Near Surface (h<<R): v<sub>o</sub> = √(g R) ≈ 7.92 km s<sup>-1</sup></b></div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>Relation: v<sub>e</sub> = √2 v<sub>o</sub> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Total Energy E = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G M m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 r</span>\n  </span></b></div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 16px; padding: 18px; margin: 26px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 14px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 Orbital Velocity & Satellite Energy Balance\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 840px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 840 480\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"earthGrad6\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#81D4FA\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"50%\" stop-color=\"#0284C7\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#0F172A\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 20)\">\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Circular Orbit &amp; Centripetal Balance</text>\n    <circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"220\" r=\"70\" fill=\"url(#earthGrad6)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"195\" cy=\"220\" rx=\"145\" ry=\"110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,4\"/>\n\n    <!-- Satellite Body -->\n    <rect x=\"330\" y=\"210\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n    <line x1=\"340\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"340\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">v_o →</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"340\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"220\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"260\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">F_g = GMm/r²</text>\n\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Relation: v_e = √2 × v_o ≈ 1.414 v_o</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"415\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"415\" y2=\"450\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(430, 20)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"390\" height=\"430\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.9)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"35\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Orbital Speed &amp; Satellite Energy</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"60\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(253,200,48,0.08)\" stroke=\"#FDC830\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"85\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FDC830\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Orbital Velocity Formula (v_o):</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">v_o = √(G M / r) = √[G M / (R + h)]</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">✓ Near Earth Surface (h ≈ 0): v_o = √(g R) ≈ 7.92 km/s</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"185\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(118,255,3,0.08)\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Satellite Mechanical Energy:</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"14.5\">E_k = G M m / (2 r) &nbsp;|&nbsp; U = - G M m / r</text>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"15.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">E_total = - G M m / (2 r) &nbsp; (Binding Energy)</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"310\" width=\"360\" height=\"105\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.08)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"335\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Geostationary Satellite Parameters:</text>\n    <text x=\"25\" y=\"362\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">• Height h ≈ 35,786 km above equator</text>\n    <text x=\"25\" y=\"385\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\">• Period T = 24 hours (Synchronous with Earth's rotation)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Circular satellite orbit showing gravitational centripetal force balance and relation v_e = √2 v_o.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- QUICK REVISION CHEAT SHEET -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">⚡ Quick Revision Cheat Sheet</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Summary Formulae & Core Relations for Unit VI: Gravitation</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14.5px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Gravitational Law &amp; G:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">F = G (m₁ m₂ / r²)<br>G = 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻²</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Acceleration due to Gravity g:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">g = G M / R² ≈ 9.8 m/s²<br>g_h = g(1 - 2h/R) &nbsp;|&nbsp; g_d = g(1 - d/R)</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Potential &amp; Energy:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">V = - G M / r &nbsp;|&nbsp; U = - G M m / r<br>Binding Energy = + G M m / (2 r)</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Kepler's Third Law:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">T² ∝ a³ &nbsp; ⇒ &nbsp; T² = (4π² / GM) a³<br>Areal Velocity dA/dt = L / (2 m)</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Escape &amp; Orbital Speeds:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">v_e = √(2 g R) ≈ 11.2 km/s<br>v_o = √(g R) ≈ 7.92 km/s &nbsp;|&nbsp; v_e = √2 v_o</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Geostationary Satellite:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">Height h ≈ 35,786 km<br>Period T = 24 hours (Equatorial Orbit)</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

export const c11Phy6HtmlSolutions = "<style>\n    p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) { text-align: justify !important; }\n    h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n    .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  </style>\n  <div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n  <h1 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2.5px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 10px;\">NCERT & Textbook Standard Exercise Solutions — Unit VI: Gravitation</h1><h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">SECTION 1: MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS (MCQs 1 - 25)</h2>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q1: The gravitational force between two point masses m₁ and m₂ separated by distance r is F. If distance is doubled, the force becomes:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) 2F</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) 4F</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) F/2</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) F/4</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (d)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> By Newton's law F ∝ 1/r². Doubling r makes force F' = F/2² = F/4.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q2: The value of universal gravitational constant G depends upon:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Nature of masses</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Medium between masses</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Temperature of environment</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Independent of all factors</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (d)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> G is a universal scalar constant independent of medium, temperature, or nature of masses.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q3: Kepler's second law (law of areas) is a direct consequence of conservation of:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Linear momentum</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Angular momentum</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Kinetic energy</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Total mass</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> Areal velocity dA/dt = L / (2m). Since central gravitational force exerts zero torque, angular momentum L is conserved.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q4: If Earth suddenly stops rotating about its axis, the value of acceleration due to gravity g at the equator will:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Increase</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Decrease</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Remain unchanged</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Become zero</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (a)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> g_eq = g - R ω². If rotation stops (ω=0), g_eq increases by R ω².</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q5: The ratio of escape velocity to orbital velocity near Earth's surface (v_e / v_o) is:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) 1</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) √2</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) 2</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) 1/√2</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> v_e = √(2gR) and v_o = √(gR). Thus v_e / v_o = √2 ≈ 1.414.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q6: The weight of a body at the center of the Earth is:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Infinite</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Equal to surface weight</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Zero</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Double the surface weight</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (c)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> At Earth center d = R, so g_d = g(1 - R/R) = 0. Weight W = m g = 0.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q7: The orbital period of a geostationary satellite is:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) 12 hours</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) 24 hours</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) 48 hours</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) 90 minutes</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> A geostationary satellite revolves with the exact same rotational period as Earth (24 hours).</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q8: Gravitational potential energy of a body of mass m at distance r from Earth center (r > R) is:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) +G M m / r</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) -G M m / r</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) -G M m / r²</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) +G M m / r²</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> U(r) = - G M m / r, taking reference U=0 at infinity.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q9: If the radius of Earth shrinks by 1% while its mass remains constant, acceleration due to gravity g on its surface will:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Increase by 1%</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Increase by 2%</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Decrease by 2%</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Decrease by 1%</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> g = G M / R². Δg/g = -2 (ΔR/R) = -2 (-1%) = +2% increase.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q10: The time period T of a simple pendulum inside an orbiting satellite is:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) 0</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) 2 seconds</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Infinite</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) 24 hours</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (c)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> In orbit, apparent gravity g_eff = 0. T = 2π √(l / g_eff) = 2π √(l/0) = ∞.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q11: Dimensions of Universal Gravitational Constant G are:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) [M¹ L³ T⁻²]</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) [M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²]</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) [M⁻¹ L² T⁻²]</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) [M¹ L² T⁻¹]</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> G = F r² / (m1 m2) ⇒ [M L T⁻²][L²]/[M²] = [M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²].</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q12: The height of a geostationary satellite above Earth's surface is approximately:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) 6400 km</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) 35,786 km</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) 10,000 km</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) 42,000 km</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> GEO altitude h = 35,786 km (or r ≈ 42,164 km from Earth center).</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q13: Escape velocity from Earth's surface depends on:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Mass of projected body</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Direction of projection</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Mass of Earth M and Radius R</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Angle of projection</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (c)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> v_e = √(2GM/R). It is independent of mass m and angle of projection.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q14: Total mechanical energy of a satellite orbiting in a circular path of radius r is:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) +G M m / 2r</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) -G M m / 2r</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) -G M m / r</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) 0</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> E_total = E_k + U = +(GMm/2r) - (GMm/r) = -GMm/2r.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q15: Polar satellites revolve around Earth in:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Equatorial plane</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) North-South polar plane</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Inclined at 45°</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Stationary path</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> Polar satellites orbit in a North-South plane passing over poles at low altitude.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q16: A body is taken from equator to pole. Its weight will:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Decrease</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Increase</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Remain constant</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Become zero</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> g is maximum at poles (g_p > g_eq due to zero rotational reduction and polar flattening). Weight increases.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q17: The work done in moving a mass m from Earth surface to infinity is:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) G M m / R</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) 2 G M m / R</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) G M m / R²</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) 0</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (a)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> W = U(∞) - U(R) = 0 - (-G M m / R) = G M m / R.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q18: Two satellites of masses m and 4m revolve in the same circular orbit of radius r. Their orbital speeds are in ratio:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) 1 : 4</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) 4 : 1</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) 1 : 1</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) 1 : 2</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (c)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> v_o = √(G M / r) is independent of satellite mass m. Ratio is 1 : 1.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q19: Gravitational force is a:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Repulsive force</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Non-conservative force</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Conservative central force</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Nuclear force</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (c)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> Gravitational force is always attractive, central, and conservative.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q20: At what height h above Earth surface does g become g/4? (R = Earth radius)</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) R/2</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) R</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) 2R</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) 4R</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> g_h = g R² / (R+h)² = g/4 ⇒ (R+h)² = 4R² ⇒ R+h = 2R ⇒ h = R.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q21: Areal velocity of a planet revolving around Sun is equal to:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) L / m</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) L / 2m</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) 2L / m</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) m / 2L</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> Kepler's 2nd law gives dA/dt = L / (2m).</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q22: Binding energy of a satellite orbiting in radius r is:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) +G M m / r</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) +G M m / 2r</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) -G M m / 2r</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) 0</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> Binding Energy = - E_total = - (- G M m / 2r) = + G M m / 2r.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q23: If Earth loses its atmosphere, the duration of day will:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Increase</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Decrease</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) Remain unchanged</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Become 12 hours</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (c)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> Mass of atmosphere is negligible compared to Earth; rotational period remains unchanged.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q24: The speed of a planet is maximum when it is at:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) Aphelion</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) Perihelion</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) End of minor axis</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) Constant speed everywhere</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (b)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> By angular momentum conservation L = m r v = const. At perihelion r is minimum, so speed v is maximum.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q25: The gravitational potential at a point on Earth's surface is V. At height h = R above surface, it is:</b>\n      <div style=\"margin: 10px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px;\">\n        <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(a) V/2</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(b) 2V</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(c) V/4</div><div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px;\">(d) V</div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer: (a)</div>\n      <div style=\"margin-top: 4px; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px;\">💡 <b>Explanation:</b> V_surface = -GM/R. At h=R, r=2R, so V' = -GM/(2R) = V/2.</div>\n    </div><h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">SECTION 2: VERY SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS (VSAs 26 - 55)</h2>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q26: State Newton's Universal Law of Gravitation.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> States that every body attracts every other body with force F = G (m₁ m₂ / r²), acting along the line joining their centers.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q27: Write the SI unit and dimensional formula of Universal Gravitational Constant G.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> SI Unit: N m² kg⁻² (or m³ kg⁻¹ s⁻²). Dimensional Formula: [M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²].</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q28: What is the acceleration due to gravity at the center of the Earth?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> Zero (g = 0), because depth d = R, giving g_d = g(1 - R/R) = 0.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q29: State Kepler's Law of Periods.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> The square of the orbital period T of a planet is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis a of its elliptical orbit (T² ∝ a³).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q30: Name the physical quantity that remains conserved in Kepler's second law.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> Angular momentum L of the revolving planet.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q31: Define gravitational potential at a point.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> The work done per unit mass in bringing a small test mass from infinity to that point in gravitational field: V = - G M / r (SI Unit: J/kg).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q32: What is the relation between escape speed v_e and orbital speed v_o near Earth surface?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> v_e = √2 v_o ≈ 1.414 v_o.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q33: What is the numerical value of escape velocity from Earth's surface?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> v_e = √(2 g R) ≈ 11.2 km/s.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q34: Why is gravitational potential energy taken as negative?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> Because gravitational force is attractive, and work is done by the field when bringing mass from infinity (where U = 0) to distance r.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q35: Define a Geostationary Satellite.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> A satellite revolving in the equatorial plane from West to East with an orbital period of exactly 24 hours, appearing stationary to Earth observers.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q36: What is the height of a geostationary satellite above Earth's surface?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> Approximately h ≈ 35,786 km (or distance r ≈ 42,164 km from Earth center).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q37: Define orbital velocity of a satellite.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> The minimum horizontal speed required to place a satellite into a stable circular orbit around Earth: v_o = √(G M / r).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q38: What is the value of orbital velocity for a low Earth satellite?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> v_o = √(g R) ≈ 7.92 km/s.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q39: What is the apparent weight of an astronaut inside an orbiting space station?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> Zero (State of Weightlessness), because normal contact reaction N = 0.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q40: Does escape speed depend on the mass of the escaping body?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> No, v_e = √(2GM/R) is completely independent of the body's mass m.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q41: What is the value of g at Earth's poles compared to equator?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> g is maximum at the poles (g_p ≈ 9.83 m/s²) and minimum at equator (g_eq ≈ 9.78 m/s²).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q42: Why does the Moon have no atmosphere?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> Because the thermal root-mean-square speed of gas molecules exceeds the small escape velocity of the Moon (v_e ≈ 2.38 km/s).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q43: Define inertial mass.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> The mass of a body measured by its resistance to acceleration under an applied force: m_i = F / a.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q44: Define gravitational mass.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> The mass of a body measured by the gravitational force exerted on it by Earth: m_g = F_g / g.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q45: What is the principle of equivalence?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> States that inertial mass m_i and gravitational mass m_g of a body are strictly equal.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q46: What is a Polar Satellite?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> A satellite orbiting in a North-South plane passing over Earth's poles at low altitude (h ≈ 500-800 km) with period T ≈ 100 min.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q47: Why are space rockets launched from West to East near equator?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> To utilize Earth's eastward rotational speed (v ≈ 465 m/s at equator) as an initial boost.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q48: What is the total mechanical energy of a satellite orbiting in radius r?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> E_total = - G M m / (2r).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q49: What does the negative sign of total energy of a satellite signify?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> It signifies that the satellite is gravitationally bound to Earth and requires energy to escape to infinity.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q50: What is the binding energy of a satellite?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> The energy required to detach the satellite from its orbit to infinity: B.E. = + G M m / (2r).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q51: How does g vary with depth d below Earth surface?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> g_d = g (1 - d/R). Acceleration g decreases linearly with depth d.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q52: How does g vary with height h above Earth surface (h << R)?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> g_h = g (1 - 2h/R).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q53: What is the direction of gravitational field intensity?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> Directively radially inward towards the center of mass creating the field.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q54: What is the work done by gravitational force in one complete planetary orbit?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> Zero, because gravitational force is conservative and displacement in a closed path is zero.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q55: Can a satellite orbit in any plane other than passing through Earth's center?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Ans:</b> No, the center of satellite's orbit must always coincide with Earth's center of mass to provide required centripetal force.</p>\n    </div><h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">SECTION 3: SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS (SAs 56 - 85)</h2>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q56: Derive the relation between acceleration due to gravity g and Universal Gravitational Constant G.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Consider a body of mass m on Earth surface (Mass M, Radius R). Gravitational force F = G M m / R². From Newton's 2nd law F = m g. Equating gives m g = G M m / R² ⇒ g = G M / R².</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q57: Derive an expression for variation of g with altitude h above Earth's surface (for h << R).</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> At surface g = G M / R². At height h, g_h = G M / (R+h)² = (G M / R²) [1 + h/R]⁻² = g (1 + h/R)⁻². Expanding binomially for h << R gives g_h = g (1 - 2h/R).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q58: Derive an expression for variation of g with depth d below Earth's surface.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Assuming Earth as a sphere of uniform density ρ, mass M = (4/3) π R³ ρ, so g = (4/3) π G ρ R. At depth d, effective mass is M' within radius (R-d). g_d = (4/3) π G ρ (R-d) = g [ (R-d) / R ] = g (1 - d/R).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q59: Differentiate between Geostationary Satellite and Polar Satellite.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> 1. GEO orbits in equatorial plane with T = 24h at h ≈ 35,786 km; used for telecommunication. 2. Polar satellite orbits over poles with T ≈ 100 min at h ≈ 500-800 km; used for weather & remote sensing.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q60: Derive the formula for Escape Speed v_e from Earth's surface.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Work done in moving mass m from R to infinity: W = ∫<sub>R</sub><sup>∞</sup> (G M m / r²) dr = G M m / R. To escape, kinetic energy ½ m v_e² must equal W: ½ m v_e² = G M m / R ⇒ v_e = √(2 G M / R) = √(2 g R).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q61: Derive the formula for Orbital Speed v_o of a satellite revolving at height h above Earth surface.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Required centripetal force is provided by gravitational force: m v_o² / (R+h) = G M m / (R+h)² ⇒ v_o = √[ G M / (R+h) ]. For low orbit (h << R), v_o = √(G M / R) = √(g R).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q62: State Kepler's three laws of planetary motion with mathematical expressions where applicable.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> 1. Law of Orbits: Elliptical orbits with Sun at focus. 2. Law of Areas: dA/dt = L / (2m) = Const. 3. Law of Periods: T² = (4π² / GM) a³ ⇒ T² ∝ a³.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q63: Explain why angular momentum is conserved in planetary motion around the Sun.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Gravitational force F is a central force acting along the radius vector r. Torque τ = r × F = 0. Since τ = dL/dt = 0, angular momentum L remains constant.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q64: Derive an expression for total energy of an orbiting satellite of mass m at radius r.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Kinetic Energy E_k = ½ m v_o² = ½ m (GM/r) = + G M m / (2r). Potential Energy U = - G M m / r. Total Energy E = E_k + U = +(GMm/2r) - (GMm/r) = - G M m / (2r).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q65: Explain the phenomenon of Weightlessness inside a satellite.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> In orbit, both satellite and astronaut experience identical centripetal acceleration g_eff towards Earth. Astronaut is in continuous free-fall, so floor reaction N = m(g - g) = 0.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q66: Calculate the height above Earth surface at which g drops to 64% of its surface value.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> g_h / g = 0.64 = (R / (R+h))² ⇒ R / (R+h) = 0.8 ⇒ R+h = 1.25 R ⇒ h = 0.25 R = 0.25 × 6400 km = 1600 km.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q67: Why is the gravitational potential at any point in a field always negative?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Because reference potential V=0 is chosen at infinity. Since gravitational force is attractive, work is done by field as mass approaches, making V negative everywhere.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q68: Show that the speed of a satellite revolving very close to Earth surface is approximately 7.92 km/s.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> v_o = √(g R) = √(9.8 m/s² × 6.4 × 10⁶ m) = √(62.72 × 10⁶) = 7920 m/s = 7.92 km/s.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q69: Show that escape velocity from Earth surface is √2 times the orbital velocity near surface.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> v_e = √(2 g R) and v_o = √(g R). Dividing v_e by v_o gives v_e / v_o = √(2 g R) / √(g R) = √2.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q70: A body weighs 63 N on Earth surface. What is its gravitational weight on Mars (mass = 1/9 Earth mass, radius = 1/2 Earth radius)?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> g_Mars / g_Earth = (M_M / M_E) × (R_E / R_M)² = (1/9) × (2)² = 4/9. Weight on Mars = 63 N × (4/9) = 28 N.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q71: Explain how g varies with latitude λ due to Earth's rotation.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Centrifugal acceleration at latitude λ is R ω² cos λ directed outward. Its component along gravity is R ω² cos² λ. Net gravity g_λ = g - R ω² cos² λ.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q72: Calculate the orbital speed of a satellite orbiting at height h = R above Earth surface.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> v_o = √[ G M / (R+h) ] = √[ G M / 2R ] = √[ g R / 2 ] = 7.92 / √2 ≈ 5.6 km/s.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q73: What is gravitational field intensity E_g? How is it related to gravitational potential V?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Intensity E_g = F/m = G M / r². Potential V = - G M / r. Relation: E_g = - dV / dr.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q74: Two artificial satellites A and B of masses m and 3m move in orbits of radii r and 2r. Compare their kinetic energies.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> E_k = G M m / (2r). E_kA = G M m / (2r). E_kB = G M (3m) / (2 × 2r) = (3/4) G M m / (2r). Ratio E_kA / E_kB = 4 / 3.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q75: Explain why two raindrops of equal mass falling towards Earth attract each other, but do not collide due to gravitation.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> Gravitational force between small masses is extremely weak (G ~ 10⁻¹¹) compared to air drag and terminal velocity dynamics.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q76: Calculate the period of revolution of a satellite orbiting near Earth surface.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> T = 2π R / v_o = 2π √(R / g) = 2π √(6.4 × 10⁶ / 9.8) ≈ 5070 seconds ≈ 84.5 minutes.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q77: State two important applications of Geostationary Satellites.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> 1. Global telecommunication and TV broadcasting. 2. Weather forecasting and meteorology.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q78: State two important applications of Polar Satellites.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> 1. Remote sensing and environmental monitoring. 2. Military reconnaissance and land mapping.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q79: Show that kinetic energy of an orbiting satellite is half the magnitude of its potential energy.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> E_k = + G M m / (2r). Potential Energy U = - G M m / r. Magnitude |U| = G M m / r. Thus E_k = ½ |U|.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q80: Why is a projection speed less than escape speed called orbital or sub-escape speed?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> If v < v_e, total energy E < 0, so the body cannot escape to infinity and remains bound in an elliptical or circular orbit.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q81: If the distance between Earth and Sun is halved, what would be the duration of the year?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> T² ∝ a³. If a' = a/2, T'² / T² = (1/2)³ = 1/8 ⇒ T' = 365 / √8 = 365 / 2.828 ≈ 129 days.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q82: Explain inertial mass vs gravitational mass with examples.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> 1. Inertial mass m_i resists acceleration (F = m_i a). 2. Gravitational mass m_g determines gravitational attraction (F_g = m_g g). Equal by Equivalence Principle.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q83: Why does a body weigh more at poles than at equator?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> 1. Earth is flattened at poles (R_p < R_eq ⇒ g = GM/R² is larger). 2. Rotational term R ω² cos² λ is zero at poles (λ=90°).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q84: Can a satellite be launched into an orbit such that it remains fixed over Delhi?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> No, Delhi is at latitude ~28.6°N. Geostationary satellites MUST orbit in the Equatorial plane (0° latitude) passing through Earth center.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 15px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Q85: What is the escape velocity of a body from a planet whose mass and radius are both double that of Earth?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Solution:</b> v_e' = √[ 2 G (2M) / (2R) ] = √(2 G M / R) = v_e = 11.2 km/s.</p>\n    </div><h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">SECTION 4: LONG ANSWER QUESTIONS (LAs 86 - 100)</h2>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q86: Derive expressions for variation of acceleration due to gravity g: (i) at height h above surface, (ii) at depth d below surface. Compare the percentage decrease in g at height h vs depth d for h = d.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Height h: g_h = g (1 - 2h/R). Percentage drop = (2h/R) × 100%.\n2. Depth d: g_d = g (1 - d/R). Percentage drop = (d/R) × 100%.\n3. Comparison for h = d: Percentage decrease at height h is TWICE the percentage decrease at depth d.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q87: State Kepler's three laws of planetary motion. Derive Kepler's third law (T² ∝ r³) for a circular orbit from Newton's law of gravitation.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. State Orbits, Areas, and Periods laws.\n2. Proof: Gravitational force provides centripetal force: G M m / r² = m v² / r ⇒ v² = G M / r.\n3. Period T = 2π r / v ⇒ T² = 4π² r² / v² = 4π² r² / (GM/r) = (4π² / GM) r³. Hence T² ∝ r³.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q88: Define Escape Velocity. Derive an expression for escape velocity of a body from Earth surface. Calculate its value for Earth and explain why Moon has no atmosphere.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Definition: Minimum speed to escape Earth gravity.\n2. Derivation: W = ∫<sub>R</sub><sup>∞</sup> (G M m / r²) dr = G M m / R. Equating ½ m v_e² = G M m / R ⇒ v_e = √(2GM/R) = √(2gR).\n3. Earth value: v_e = √(2 × 9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶) = 11.2 km/s.\n4. Moon: v_e_Moon ≈ 2.38 km/s, which is less than thermal rms speed of atmospheric gas molecules, causing gas to escape.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q89: Define Orbital Velocity of a satellite. Derive expressions for: (i) Orbital Speed v_o, (ii) Period of revolution T, (iii) Height h of geostationary satellite above Earth.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. v_o = √(GM / (R+h)).\n2. T = 2π (R+h) / v_o = 2π (R+h)³/² / √(GM).\n3. Height h of GEO: T = 24h = 86400s. Substituting T² = (4π²/GM)(R+h)³ yields (R+h) ≈ 42,164 km ⇒ h = 42,164 - 6400 = 35,764 km ≈ 35,786 km.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q90: Derive expressions for Kinetic Energy, Potential Energy, Total Mechanical Energy, and Binding Energy of a satellite revolving around Earth in a circular orbit of radius r. Draw energy graph.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. E_k = + G M m / (2r).\n2. U = - G M m / r.\n3. E_total = E_k + U = - G M m / (2r).\n4. Binding Energy B.E. = + G M m / (2r).\n5. Graph analysis: E_k is positive parabola, U is negative parabola, E_total is negative curve symmetrical to E_k.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q91: What is a Geostationary Satellite? State essential conditions for a satellite to be geostationary. Calculate its orbital radius and speed.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Definition & 4 Conditions: Equatorial plane, West to East, T = 24h, fixed height h ≈ 35,786 km.\n2. Radius r = [ G M T² / 4π² ]¹/³ = [ (6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 6×10²⁴ × 86400²) / 39.48 ]¹/³ ≈ 42,200 km.\n3. Orbital speed v_o = 2π r / T = 2π × 42200 / 86400 ≈ 3.07 km/s.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q92: What is meant by Gravitational Potential and Gravitational Potential Energy? Derive expressions for both at a distance r from Earth center (r > R).</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Definitions of V and U.\n2. Derivation of V: V = W/m = ∫<sub>∞</sub><sup>r</sup> (-GM/x²) dx = [-GM/x]<sub>∞</sub><sup>r</sup> = -GM/r.\n3. Derivation of U: U = m V = - G M m / r.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q93: Explain how acceleration due to gravity g varies with: (i) Altitude h, (ii) Depth d, (iii) Latitude λ. Find latitude λ where weight of a body becomes zero if Earth rotates at critical speed.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Detail 3 formulas.\n2. Weight zero condition: g_λ = 0 ⇒ g - R ω² cos² λ = 0 ⇒ cos λ = √(g / (R ω²)). At equator λ=0°, weight is zero when ω = √(g/R).</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q94: Explain the concept of Weightlessness in an artificial satellite. Is gravity zero inside an orbiting satellite? Why do astronauts float freely?</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Gravity is NOT zero (g_orbit ≈ 0.88 g_surface at 400 km LEO).\n2. Both spacecraft and astronaut fall towards Earth with exact same acceleration a = g_orbit.\n3. Floor reaction N = m(g - a) = 0. Zero normal reaction causes floating weightlessness.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q95: Two heavy spheres of mass 100 kg and 1000 kg and radius 0.1 m and 0.2 m are placed 1.0 m apart. Find: (i) Gravitational field at midpoint of line joining centers, (ii) Gravitational potential at midpoint.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Midpoint distance r1 = r2 = 0.5 m.\n2. Field E = E2 - E1 = G(1000)/(0.5)² - G(100)/(0.5)² = 4000 G - 400 G = 3600 G = 3600 × 6.674×10⁻¹¹ = 2.4 × 10⁻⁷ N/kg towards 1000 kg sphere.\n3. Potential V = V1 + V2 = -G(100)/0.5 - G(1000)/0.5 = -2200 G = -1.47 × 10⁻⁷ J/kg.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q96: A satellite of mass m = 200 kg orbits Earth at height h = 500 km. Find: (i) Orbital speed, (ii) Kinetic energy, (iii) Potential energy, (iv) Total energy, (v) Escape speed from this orbit.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Radius r = 6400 + 500 = 6900 km = 6.9 × 10⁶ m.\n2. v_o = √(GM/r) = √(4×10¹⁴ / 6.9×10⁶) = 7.61 km/s.\n3. E_k = ½ m v_o² = ½ × 200 × (7610)² = 5.79 × 10⁹ J.\n4. U = -2 E_k = -11.58 × 10⁹ J.\n5. E_total = -5.79 × 10⁹ J.\n6. v_e_orbit = √2 v_o = 1.414 × 7.61 = 10.76 km/s.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q97: A rocket is fired vertically from Earth surface with speed v = 0.6 v_e. Find the maximum height h reached by rocket in terms of Earth radius R.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Initial E = ½ m (0.6 v_e)² - G M m / R = ½ m (0.36 × 2GM/R) - GMm/R = -0.64 GMm/R.\n2. At max height h: E' = -GMm / (R+h).\n3. Equating: -GMm / (R+h) = -0.64 GMm/R ⇒ R+h = R / 0.64 = 1.5625 R ⇒ h = 0.5625 R = (9/16) R.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q98: State and prove the Principle of Superposition of Gravitational Forces. Calculate net force on mass m at vertex of an equilateral triangle of side a with masses m at all 3 vertices.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Superposition Principle: Net force on mass m is vector sum of forces exerted by all other individual masses: F_net = ∑ F_i.\n2. Triangle setup: Mass at A experiences F_B = G m² / a² at 60° to F_C = G m² / a².\n3. Resultant F_net = √(F² + F² + 2 F² cos 60°) = F √3 = √3 G m² / a² directed along angle bisector.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q99: Discuss the launching of an artificial satellite into orbit around Earth using a two-stage rocket. Explain why a single stage rocket cannot put a satellite into orbit.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Stage 1 takes rocket vertically through thick atmosphere to desired height h.\n2. Stage 2 tilts horizontally and fires to impart horizontal orbital speed v_o = √(GM/(R+h)).\n3. Single stage rocket cannot turn 90° in mid-air to provide pure horizontal orbital velocity without secondary thrust vectoring.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.04); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Q100: Calculate the energy required to transfer a 400 kg satellite from a circular orbit of radius 2R to a circular orbit of radius 4R around Earth.</b>\n      <p style=\"margin: 8px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-line;\"><b>Comprehensive Solution:</b>\n1. Initial Energy E₁ = - G M m / (2 × 2R) = - G M m / (4R).\n2. Final Energy E₂ = - G M m / (2 × 4R) = - G M m / (8R).\n3. Energy Required ΔE = E₂ - E₁ = (-GMm/8R) - (-GMm/4R) = + G M m / (8R) = (1/8) m g R = (1/8) × 400 × 9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶ = 3.136 × 10⁹ Joules.</p>\n    </div></div>";
